@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  supported child host, parent adapter, child host, parent model,
}
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import { starter tools }

component AgentConfig {
  goal {
    Load and validate the spike's agent configuration so that every other
    component receives an agent definition whose child host, model and
    thinking level come from one model tier and whose skills are absolute
    folders.
  }

  state {
    The configuration is one *agent file*, .agents/subzero.yaml, with a
    *skills map* from skill name to *skill folder*, an *endpoints map*, a
    *models map* and an *agents map* of named agents, each an *agent entry*.

    The models map holds a *tiers map* that gives each *model tier* by name a
    child host, which the agent file writes under the key host, a *model* and a
    *thinking level*. The child host, the model and the thinking level live
    only in a model tier or a tier override, and never directly in an
    agent entry.

    The *medium tier* is the model tier named medium. It is what a request runs
    on when it names no model tier and the agent entry names none of its own,
    so the agent file must hold it.

    An agent entry has a *description*, the names of its skills, a *tool list*
    and a *timeout*, and may name a model tier as its own tier and hold a
    *tier override* for any model tier. The entries named general and fallback
    have
    no description. A tier override replaces, for that agent only, any of the
    child host, the model and the thinking level of the model tier it names,
    and leaves the rest of that model tier as it is.

    An *agent definition* is one agent entry resolved at one model tier: the
    tier that the request names, or else the entry's own tier, or else the
    medium tier. It has the description, the skills, the tool list and the
    timeout of the entry, and the child host, the model and the thinking level
    of that model tier after the entry's tier override for it. It is named
    after the entry, and named <entry>:<tier> when the request names a
    model tier.

    The endpoints map holds an *endpoints entry* for a *model endpoint*. A
    model endpoint is the part of a model before its first slash, and each
    agent definition belongs to the model endpoint of its model. The entry
    holds the *parallel limit* of that model endpoint, a positive whole number
    that is the most child runs that one process runs at the same time on it. A
    model endpoint with no entry has no parallel limit, and every
    agent definition on the same model endpoint shares its parallel limit.

    The timeout is a required number of seconds. It limits the wall-clock time
    of a child run from its spawn to its settlement, including
    skill verification, and it does not run while the child run is Pending.

    The agents map must hold an entry named general, the *general agent*, which
    takes work that names no agent or names general, and whose tool list and
    timeout tiered work uses. The agents map must also hold an entry named
    fallback, the *fallback agent*, which takes the work of a name other than
    general that the agents map does not hold. Its tool list can be limited to
    tools that only read, and a front end's init writes it that way. Neither
    the general agent nor the fallback agent is chosen by its description or
    offered to a parent model for routing. The medium tier, the general agent
    and the fallback agent that a front end's init adds are the
    *starter entries*.

    A *routable tier* is a model tier whose child host, after the tier override
    of the general agent, is a supported child host, or any model tier when the
    caller gives no supported child hosts. The medium tier is a routable tier
    on the same terms as any other, and a front end that routes by tier word
    leaves out a routable tier that has no tier word.

    A *tier word* is how a compound-engineering skill names the model of a
    generic subagent. The tier word cheapest capable maps to the model tier
    small, mid-tier maps to medium, and session model maps to large. The
    tier word session model is the name that compound-engineering uses for the
    model tier large. It is not a fallback to the model of the parent session,
    and
    no request runs on it unless it names it.

    A *working directory* is the directory in which a session of a parent host
    or a command was started.

    A *tier text* is the model tier that a parent model writes in a request,
    which may be empty. An *agent alias* is a name that a parent model may
    write for the general agent: delegate and general are the agent aliases.
  }

  logic {
    On load, read the agent file, validate every model tier, every agent entry,
    the general agent, the fallback agent and the medium tier included, every
    tier override against the *agent-file schema*, which consists only of the
    validation rules in this AgentConfig contract, and resolve each
    skill folder relative to the directory of the agent file into an absolute
    path. The flow returns each agent entry, each model tier and the
    names of each routable tier to its caller, or stops with a *load error* that
    names
    the offending entry or model tier, or names the missing general agent,
    fallback agent or medium tier, or says that the agent file is missing,
    unreadable or malformed. One invalid model tier or agent entry fails the
    whole load.

    On a request for an agent, resolve the agent entry for the agent name, or
    the general agent when the request names no agent or an agent alias. A name
    that the agents map does not hold stops the flow with the
    unknown agent error. Then choose the model tier: the tier the request
    names, or else the entry's own tier, or else the medium tier. Apply the
    entry's tier override for that tier and return the agent definition, which
    ends the flow. When the chosen tier is not configured, or its child host
    after the override is not a supported child host, return the tier error and
    end the flow.

    On a tier text, return the model tier it maps to, which ends the flow: a
    configured model tier name, or tier: followed by one, maps to that
    model tier, and a tier word maps to its model tier. An empty tier text maps
    to no model tier, and any other tier text is an *unmatched tier text*,
    which the caller treats as an empty tier text.
  }

  constraints {
    A model tier must be rejected with a load error that names it when it has
    no child host, no model or no thinking level. A tier override must be
    rejected with a load error that names its agent and its model tier when it
    names a model tier that is not configured or holds anything other than a
    child host, a model or a thinking level. An agent entry must be rejected,
    naming it, when its own tier is not a configured model tier, or when it
    holds a child host, a model or a thinking level directly.

    The agent file must be rejected with a load error that names the
    general agent when it holds no general agent, names the fallback agent when
    it holds no fallback agent, and names the medium tier when its models map
    holds no model tier named medium. A file must be rejected for a missing
    general agent or fallback agent even when it holds no other agent entry.

    AgentConfig takes the supported child hosts from its caller, which gets
    them from Kernel, and AgentConfig names no child host itself. When its
    caller gives no supported child hosts, AgentConfig checks no child host. A
    model tier whose child host is not a supported child host still loads and
    is not a routable tier.

    The general agent and the fallback agent must each be rejected with a
    load error that starts with its name when it holds a description, because
    neither is chosen by its description.

    An endpoints entry must be rejected with a load error that names its
    model endpoint when its parallel limit is not a positive whole number, or
    when no model of a model tier or of a tier override belongs to its
    model endpoint.

    An agent entry must be rejected, naming it, when it has no timeout or its
    timeout is not a positive number of seconds.

    An agent entry that names a skill missing from the skills map must be
    rejected with the agent and the skill both named.

    The fallback agent must keep the tool list that the agent file gives it, at
    every model tier, and its tool list must not change the tool list of the
    general agent. AgentConfig names no tool, because tool names belong to the
    child host.

    A skill folder that does not exist on disk must still load. The missing
    folder is reported only when a child is launched.

    A request that resolves an agent at a model tier that is not configured, or
    whose child host after the tier override is not a supported child host,
    must return a *tier error* that names the tier it resolved to, lists every
    configured model tier and, when the child host is the cause, names that
    child host and every supported child host, and it must not fail the load.
    The listing of each routable tier names a model tier that the
    general agent can run, for the tool description and the hook, and whether
    any agent runs at a model tier is decided by the child host of its resolved
    agent definition.

    AgentConfig must read the agent file again on each call that asks for an
    agent definition or a model tier, so that an edit to
    it is picked up without a restart.

    AgentConfig must read only the one agent file, and it writes that file only
    to add the starter entries that its interface describes. It must not merge
    layers, write *subzero.lock* or ask for *repo trust* in the spike, because
    those belong to the agent definition format of a later version.

    When it adds the starter entries, AgentConfig must leave every existing
    entry of the agent file unchanged, and leave each existing line unchanged
    except when adding to an empty or flow-style map requires rewriting that
    map. Add only the medium tier, the general agent or the fallback agent when
    the agent file does not hold it yet, write nothing when the file exists and
    does not load for a reason other than a missing starter entry, and write
    entries that load.

    AgentConfig resolves the *project root* from *SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR* first,
    then from the roots that the parent adapter reports, and for the command
    line from its working directory. It must never use the working directory of
    the process that runs it, except for the command line. It keeps no
    project root between calls, because DelegationService asks once for each
    DelegationService and hands the answer to the other components. It finds
    the agent file under the project root that its caller hands over.

    A caller may ask AgentConfig to resolve the project root from the roots
    that its parent adapter reports alone, and then SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR is not
    read.

    An empty SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR counts as unset. When the parent adapter
    reports several roots, the first one reported wins. The project root must
    be an absolute path with its symbolic links resolved. When no source gives
    a project root, AgentConfig must return an *unresolved root error* that
    names SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR as the setting to fix, and must resolve again on
    the next call.
  }

  cases {
    A file with the medium tier set to the child host pi, the model
    r9700/Qwen3.8 and the thinking level medium, a general agent, a
    fallback agent and one agent entry with no tier of its own, loads, and that
    agent definition has that child host, model and thinking level and absolute
    skill folder entries.

    With the model tiers medium, small and large configured, the general agent
    resolves to four agent definitions: general on the medium tier, and
    general:small, general:medium and general:large on the tier each names.

    A reviewer entry whose own tier is small resolves to the small model tier
    when the request names no tier, and to the large model tier, named
    reviewer:large, when the request names large. A tier override of the
    reviewer for large that sets only the model keeps the child host and the
    thinking level of large.

    An agent entry that holds a model directly gives a load error that names
    it, and so does a tier override that names a model tier that is not
    configured.

    A file that holds an agent entry but no general agent gives a load error
    that names the general agent, and a file with a general agent and no
    fallback agent gives a load error that names the fallback agent. A file
    with no model tier named medium gives a load error that names the
    medium tier.

    A model tier large with the child host claude, when only pi is a
    supported child host, loads, is not a routable tier, and a request for it
    gives the tier error that names claude and pi. When the caller gives no
    supported child hosts, large is a routable tier.

    A name that is not configured gives an unknown agent error that lists every
    configured agent entry. AgentConfig only reports the error, and
    DelegationService decides whether the fallback agent runs instead.

    Adding the starter entries to an agent file that holds general and fallback
    agents and no medium tier adds the medium tier and leaves every existing
    entry as it was. Adding them where no agent file exists creates the file
    with all three.

    With no SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR and no root reported by the parent adapter, the
    project root is not resolved and the caller gets the unresolved root error.

    Two agent definitions on the model endpoint r9700, whose entry has a
    parallel limit of 1, are both given that parallel limit of 1, and an
    agent definition on a model endpoint with no entry is given none. An entry
    for r9770 that no model of a model tier or tier override belongs to gives a
    load error that names r9770.

    An agent file with one invalid agent entry gives a load error that names it
    and returns no agent definition, not even a valid one.

    A fallback agent that holds a description gives a load error that names it.
    With a general agent whose tool list holds read, write and bash and a
    fallback agent whose tool list holds only read, the fallback agent holds
    only read at every model tier, and general:medium keeps read, write and
    bash.

    With the model tiers small and large configured, the tier text small and
    tier:small map to small, the tier word cheapest capable maps to small, the
    tier word session model maps to large, the tier text anthropic/claude-opus
    is an unmatched tier text, and an empty tier text maps to no model tier.
  }

  interface {
    Resolve an agent entry of the agents map by name at a requested model tier,
    at its own tier or at the medium tier, and provide the agent definition
    with its child host, its model, its thinking level, its tool list, its
    timeout, its model endpoint with the parallel limit of that model endpoint
    or none, and every skill folder resolved to an absolute folder.

    Report which starter entries it added and which the agent file already
    held, and add the starter entries to the agent file under a project root
    that its caller hands over: the medium tier from a child host, a model and
    a thinking level, and the general agent and the fallback agent from the
    starter tools that the caller gives and a timeout, each only when the
    agent file does not hold it yet, with no skills, creating the agent file,
    its models map and its agents map when missing. Refuse with the load error
    when the file exists and does not load for a reason other than a missing
    starter entry, and refuse with an error that names both child hosts,
    writing nothing, when the file already holds a medium tier whose child host
    differs from the child host that the caller gives.

    List the routable tier names, the medium tier among them, with the
    child host and the model each one runs for the general agent, apart from
    the names of every configured model tier.

    Map a tier text to a model tier, including each tier word, or report an
    unmatched tier text, and treat each agent alias as the general agent, so
    that every parent adapter maps a request the same way.

    List the names of every agent entry of the agents map so that a caller can
    report them in an error.

    Return an *unknown agent error* that names the unknown name and every
    configured agent entry when a name is not configured, so that a front end
    can show it. When no agent is named, the error says that no agent was named
    and still lists every configured agent entry.

    Return the tier error that names the model tier an agent resolved to, every
    configured model tier and, when the child host is the cause, that
    child host and every supported child host, when the agent cannot run at
    that model tier.

    Return a load error that names the invalid model tier or agent entry, the
    missing general agent, fallback agent or medium tier, or says that the
    agent file is missing, unreadable or malformed, so that a front end can
    show it.

    Provide the project root for a caller in the order the constraints give,
    starting from SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR, then the roots that the parent adapter
    reports, then the working directory of the command line, or return the
    unresolved root error when no source gives one.
  }

  decisions {
    Each skill folder resolves against the directory of the agent file and not the
    working directory, so the same file behaves the same from a parent adapter,
    the command line and the experiment harness.

    The child host, the model and the thinking level live in model tiers and
    not in agent entries, so that what runs a child is set once per tier and an
    agent says only what it does. An agent that needs something else at one
    tier gets a tier override for it, as a docker-compose override replaces
    fields of a base service, and an agent that should run on another tier by
    default names it as its own tier. The medium tier is required so that every
    agent entry resolves without a tier named, and the general agent and the
    fallback agent are required so that every request has an agent to run and a
    read-only agent for a mistyped name.

    The general agent and the fallback agent are entries of the agents map that
    are named general and fallback, so that a name lookup, a tier and an
    explicit request find them like any agent and no agent name is reserved.
    They have no description, because the general agent is the default for work
    that names no agent and for tiered work, and neither is chosen by its
    description or offered to a parent model for routing. The fallback agent
    takes the work of a name other than general that the agents map does not
    hold, and a front end's init writes it read-only so that a mistyped agent
    name cannot write to the repository or run shell commands, while tiered
    work keeps the tools of the general agent for compound-engineering
    reviewers. Only the child host knows which tool names only read, so the
    load cannot enforce read-only, and a person who edits the entry owns that
    change.

    The tier word and the tier text mapping live here and not in a
    parent adapter, so that the Claude Code parent host and the pi parent host
    map a request the same way. A pi project with no agent file gets a
    load error that says to run subzero init, so that no subagent runs on a
    model that the agent file does not name.

    A parallel limit keeps a fan-out on one model endpoint from flooding it.
  }
}
